<date>2026-11-13</date>
<releaseinfo>0.1.0</releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>
&tdenmctl; (<command>tde-nmctl</command>) manages network connections in
the Trinity Desktop Environment through &nm;. It consists of a system tray
applet for connecting to wired, Wi-Fi, VPN and mobile networks, and a
connection editor that is also a Trinity Control Center module and a
System Settings module. Passwords are kept in &wallet;.
</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>TDE</keyword>
<keyword>Trinity</keyword>
<keyword>NetworkManager</keyword>
<keyword>network</keyword>
<keyword>Wi-Fi</keyword>
<keyword>wireless</keyword>
<keyword>VPN</keyword>
<keyword>WireGuard</keyword>
<keyword>OpenVPN</keyword>
</keywordset>
</bookinfo>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>
&nm; is the service that brings up the network connections of most Linux
systems: it connects to wired networks and Wi-Fi networks, starts VPN
connections and mobile broadband, gets the addresses by DHCP and sets the
DNS servers. A desktop program tells &nm; which networks to use and gives
it the passwords.
</para>

<para>
&tdenmctl; is such a program for TDE. It is modelled on the programs that
other desktops use with &nm;: <application>nm-applet</application> and
<application>nm-connection-editor</application> (network-manager-gnome),
<application>nm-tray</application> and <application>plasma-nm</application>.
It has three parts:
</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>the <link linkend="tray">tray applet</link>
(<command>tde-nmctl-tray</command>), which shows the network state in the
panel, lists the Wi-Fi networks in range and connects with one click. It
also hands the passwords stored in &wallet; to &nm; and asks for missing
ones;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>the <link linkend="connections">connection editor</link>
(<command>tde-nmctl</command>), which adds, changes and deletes connection
profiles of every kind, shows the network devices and has the global
settings;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>the same editor as a <link linkend="kcm">Control Center and
System Settings module</link>.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>
TDE also ships <application>TDENetworkManager</application>, an older
applet that supports fewer of &nm;'s features. &tdenmctl; talks to &nm;
directly over D-Bus and supports almost all of them. When both start at
login, the tray asks once whether TDENetworkManager's autostart should be
turned off.
</para>

<note><title>Vibecode disclaimer</title>
<para>
&tdenmctl; was written largely by an AI coding assistant, with a person
giving direction (it was <quote>vibe coded</quote>). It has been tested
against a real &nm; and a simulated one, but the code has not been read
line by line by a person. It changes how your computer connects to
networks and handles your network passwords, so use it at your own risk.
It comes with no warranty of any kind. Bug reports and reviews are very
welcome at <ulink url="https://github.com/Yjlion/tde-nmctl/issues">the
project's issue tracker</ulink>.
</para>
</note>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="tray">
<title>The Tray Applet</title>

<para>
<command>tde-nmctl-tray</command> starts at login and shows an icon in the
system tray of the panel. The icon shows the primary connection, the one
that carries the default route:
</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>a network socket for a wired connection, with a warning
sign if the network has no Internet access;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>a fan of four bars for Wi-Fi, filled according to the
signal strength;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>bars for mobile broadband;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>a padlock in the corner while a VPN or WireGuard connection
is up;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>dots while connecting, a red cross when not connected and a
crossed out fan when networking is turned off.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>
The tooltip lists the active connections with their devices and the Wi-Fi
signal strength.
</para>

<sect1 id="tray-popup">
<title>The Popup</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The tray popup</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="tray-popup.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The tray popup with active connections and Wi-Fi networks</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
A left click on the icon opens the popup. From top to bottom it shows:
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term>State</term>
<listitem><para>The primary connection and whether the Internet can be
reached (&nm;'s connectivity check): <quote>Full Internet access</quote>,
<quote>Limited</quote> or <quote>Behind a captive portal</quote>, which
means you have to sign in on a web page first.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Networking</guilabel>, <guilabel>Wi-Fi</guilabel>, <guilabel>Mobile</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Turn all networking, the Wi-Fi radio and mobile broadband
on and off. Wi-Fi is only shown if there is a Wi-Fi device, Mobile only if
there is a modem. A switch is disabled if the device is turned off by a
hardware switch.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Active Connections</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Each connection that is up or coming up, with its device,
address, speed or signal strength. The <guiicon>i</guiicon> button opens the
<link linkend="tray-info">connection information</link>,
<guibutton>Disconnect</guibutton> closes the connection.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Wi-Fi Networks</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The networks in range, one line per network name, with
saved networks first and then by signal strength. Each line shows the
security (a padlock and WPA2, WPA3, WEP, Enterprise or Open), whether a
connection is <quote>Saved</quote> and the band. The refresh button asks
the Wi-Fi device to search again; while the popup is open this also
happens every 20 seconds. <guibutton>Connect</guibutton> connects, see
<xref linkend="tray-connect"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Connect to Hidden Network</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Opens a dialog for a network that does not broadcast its
name: enter the name (SSID), the security, the password and the
device.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>VPN and Other Connections</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The VPN and WireGuard connections and the other saved
connections that can be started now, for example a second wired profile
or a bridge.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Edit Connections...</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Starts the <link linkend="connections">connection
editor</link>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tray-connect">
<title>Connecting to a Wi-Fi Network</title>

<para>
When you press <guibutton>Connect</guibutton> next to a Wi-Fi network:
</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>If a connection for this network is saved, the most
recently used one is started.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>An open network is connected at once.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>For a network with a password (WPA, WPA2, WPA3 or WEP) the
password dialog opens. With <guilabel>Remember in TDE Wallet</guilabel> the
password is kept in &wallet; and the network is connected automatically
from then on.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>An enterprise network (WPA2 Enterprise, 802.1X) needs more
than a password, so the connection editor opens on its
<guilabel>Wi-Fi Security</guilabel> tab; see <xref linkend="edit-8021x"/>.
The network is connected when you press <guibutton>OK</guibutton>.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The password dialog</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="password-dialog.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The dialog that asks for a Wi-Fi password</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
A connection created this way is for your user only, unless you are
allowed to change connections for all users without a password. You can
change this later in the editor's <guilabel>General</guilabel> tab.
</para>

<para>
The same dialog opens whenever &nm; needs a password that is not stored:
a VPN password that is to be asked for every time, a changed Wi-Fi
password, or a SIM PIN.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tray-info">
<title>Connection Information</title>

<para>
<guimenuitem>Connection Information...</guimenuitem> in the context menu,
or the <guiicon>i</guiicon> button of an active connection, shows one tab
per active connection with the device, driver, hardware address, speed or
bit rate, the Wi-Fi network, signal, security, frequency and access point,
the IPv4 and IPv6 addresses, gateway and DNS servers, and the traffic
received and sent with the current rate.
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Connection information</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="info-dialog.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The connection information dialog</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tray-menu">
<title>The Context Menu</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The tray menu</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="tray-menu.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The context menu of the tray icon</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Enable Networking</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Enable Wi-Fi</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Enable Mobile Broadband</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>The same switches as in the popup.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Connection Information...</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>See <xref linkend="tray-info"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Connect to Hidden Wi-Fi Network...</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>For networks that do not broadcast their name.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Edit Connections...</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Starts the connection editor.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Show Notifications</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Notifications when a connection is established, lost or
fails, and when a VPN connects or disconnects. How they are shown (popup,
sound, ...) is set in the Control Center under
<guimenuitem>System Notifications</guimenuitem>, application
<quote>Network</quote>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Start Automatically at Login</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Untick to stop the tray from starting at login. Note that
the tray stores your passwords' access: without it, &nm; cannot get
passwords from &wallet; (connections whose passwords are stored for all
users still work).</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="connections">
<title>The Connection Editor</title>

<para>
Start the editor with <guibutton>Edit Connections...</guibutton> in the
tray, from the K menu (<guimenuitem>Settings</guimenuitem> &gt;
<guimenuitem>Network Connections</guimenuitem>) or with
<command>tde-nmctl</command>. It has three tabs:
<guilabel>Connections</guilabel>, <guilabel>Devices</guilabel> and
<guilabel>Settings</guilabel>.
</para>

<sect1 id="connections-list">
<title>The List of Connections</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The connection list</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="connections.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The list of connections</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
A <emphasis>connection</emphasis> (or connection profile) is a set of
settings for one network: the Wi-Fi network at home, the office network
with its 802.1X login, a VPN. &nm; picks a suitable connection when a
device becomes available, or starts the one you choose.
</para>

<para>
The list groups the connections as Wired, Wi-Fi, VPN, Mobile and DSL, and
Virtual (bonds, bridges, VLANs...). The <guilabel>State</guilabel> column
shows the active ones with their device, <guilabel>Last Used</guilabel>
when they were last connected. The buttons:
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Add</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>A menu of the connection types; choose one to open the
editor for a new connection. <guimenuitem>Wi-Fi Hotspot</guimenuitem>
creates a Wi-Fi access point that shares this computer's Internet
connection.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Edit...</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Opens the selected connection in the editor (also a
double click).</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Delete</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Deletes the connection, and its passwords from
&wallet;.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Duplicate</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Makes a copy with a new name and UUID, including the
passwords.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Connect</guibutton> / <guibutton>Disconnect</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Starts or stops the selected connection.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Import VPN...</guibutton>, <guibutton>Export...</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Imports an OpenVPN (<filename>.ovpn</filename>), WireGuard
or vpnc configuration file as a new connection, or exports a VPN
connection to a file. This uses the VPN plugins through
<command>nmcli</command>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>
The line above the list says when changes need the administrator's
password, see <xref linkend="security-polkit"/>.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="editing">
<title>Editing a Connection</title>

<para>
The editor has a tab for each group of settings; which tabs there are
depends on the type of connection. Press <guibutton>OK</guibutton> to save:
the editor checks the input first and shows the tab with the problem if
something is missing or wrong. Settings the editor does not show are kept
as they are.
</para>

<sect2 id="edit-general">
<title>General</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The General tab</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="editor-general.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The General tab of the editor</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Connection name</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The name shown in the lists.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Connect automatically with priority</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>&nm; starts the connection by itself when its device and
network are available. When several could be used, the one with the
highest priority wins.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Connection attempts</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>How often &nm; tries to connect automatically before giving
up.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>All users may connect to this network</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Without it, only your user can see and use the connection.
Changing a connection for all users usually needs the administrator's
password.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Automatically connect to VPN</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Starts the chosen VPN whenever this connection comes
up.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Firewall zone</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The firewalld zone of the interface while this connection
is used, for example <quote>home</quote> or <quote>public</quote>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Metered connection</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Tells applications that data costs money on this
connection.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="edit-ethernet">
<title>Ethernet</title>
<para>
<guilabel>Device</guilabel> ties the connection to one network interface
(or <quote>(any)</quote>); <guilabel>Restrict to MAC address</guilabel> to
one network card. <guilabel>Cloned MAC address</guilabel> sets the address
the card uses on the network: an address, or <guilabel>Preserve</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Permanent</guilabel>, <guilabel>Random</guilabel> (new for each
connection) or <guilabel>Stable</guilabel> (random, but the same for this
connection). <guilabel>MTU</guilabel>, <guilabel>Link negotiation</guilabel>
with speed and duplex, and <guilabel>Wake on LAN</guilabel> are for
special cases.
</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="edit-wifi">
<title>Wi-Fi</title>
<para>
<guilabel>SSID</guilabel> is the name of the network. <guilabel>Mode</guilabel>
is <guilabel>Client</guilabel> for a normal network, or
<guilabel>Hotspot</guilabel> to make this computer an access point that
shares its connection (the IPv4 method becomes <quote>Shared to other
computers</quote>), <guilabel>Ad-hoc</guilabel> or <guilabel>Mesh</guilabel>.
<guilabel>Band</guilabel> and <guilabel>Channel</guilabel> restrict the
frequencies; <guilabel>BSSID</guilabel> ties the connection to one access
point. <guilabel>MAC address randomization</guilabel>, <guilabel>Power
saving</guilabel> and <guilabel>This is a hidden network</guilabel> are
also here.
</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="edit-wifi-security">
<title>Wi-Fi Security</title>

<para>
<guilabel>Security</guilabel> has the methods of nm-connection-editor:
<guilabel>None</guilabel>, <guilabel>WEP</guilabel> with a key or a
passphrase, <guilabel>LEAP</guilabel>, <guilabel>Dynamic WEP
(802.1X)</guilabel>, <guilabel>WPA &amp; WPA2 Personal</guilabel>,
<guilabel>WPA3 Personal</guilabel> (SAE), <guilabel>WPA &amp; WPA2
Enterprise</guilabel>, <guilabel>WPA3 Enterprise</guilabel> and
<guilabel>Enhanced Open (OWE)</guilabel>. For personal networks enter the
password; see <xref linkend="security-passwords"/> for where it is
stored. <guilabel>Protected management frames</guilabel> can be required
or turned off for networks that need it.
</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="edit-8021x">
<title>802.1X Security (Enterprise Networks)</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>802.1X settings</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="editor-wifi-security.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>WPA2 Enterprise settings with PEAP</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
Enterprise Wi-Fi networks and some wired networks (the
<guilabel>802.1X Security</guilabel> tab of a wired connection) check a
user name and password or a certificate. Your network administrator tells
you which <guilabel>Authentication</guilabel> to use:
</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Protected EAP (PEAP)</guilabel> and
<guilabel>Tunneled TLS (TTLS)</guilabel>: a user name
(<guilabel>Identity</guilabel>) and password inside an encrypted tunnel.
<guilabel>Inner authentication</guilabel> is usually MSCHAPv2 for PEAP and
PAP or MSCHAPv2 for TTLS.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>TLS</guilabel>: a user certificate and its
private key.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>PWD</guilabel>, <guilabel>LEAP</guilabel>,
<guilabel>FAST</guilabel> and <guilabel>MD5</guilabel> are
rarer.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
<para>
Set the <guilabel>CA certificate</guilabel> of the network and the
<guilabel>Domain</guilabel> of its server: without them, anyone can set
up a network of the same name and collect your password. Only tick
<guilabel>No CA certificate is required</guilabel> if there is no other
way.
</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="edit-ip">
<title>IPv4 and IPv6 Settings</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>IPv4 settings</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="editor-ipv4.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>IPv4 settings with a manual address</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Method</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Automatic</guilabel> gets the address by DHCP
(and for IPv6 also by router advertisements); <guilabel>Manual</guilabel>
uses the addresses in the table; <guilabel>Link-local only</guilabel> uses
an automatic address without a server; <guilabel>Shared to other
computers</guilabel> gives addresses to other computers and shares this
computer's connection with them; <guilabel>Disabled</guilabel> (and for
IPv6 <guilabel>Ignore</guilabel>) does not configure this protocol.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Addresses</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>For the manual method: address, netmask or prefix length
(<userinput>255.255.255.0</userinput> or <userinput>24</userinput>) and
gateway. Only one gateway is used.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>DNS servers</guilabel>, <guilabel>Search domains</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Separated by commas or spaces. With
<guilabel>Use automatically obtained DNS servers</guilabel> unticked only
these are used.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>DHCP client ID</guilabel>, <guilabel>DHCP host name</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>What is sent to the DHCP server.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Use this connection only for resources on its network</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Do not route everything through this connection, only its
own network and its routes (useful for a VPN).</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Require IPv4 addressing for this connection to complete</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Without it, the connection counts as up if only IPv6
works (or the other way round).</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guibutton>Routes...</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Static routes: destination, prefix, gateway and
metric.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>IPv6 privacy extensions</guilabel>, <guilabel>Address generation</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Temporary IPv6 addresses for outgoing connections, and how
the permanent address is formed.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="edit-vpn">
<title>VPN</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>VPN settings</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="editor-vpn.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>OpenVPN settings</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
&nm; connects to VPNs through plugins, which are separate packages:
<filename>network-manager-openvpn</filename>,
<filename>network-manager-vpnc</filename> (Cisco compatible),
<filename>network-manager-openconnect</filename> (Cisco AnyConnect,
GlobalProtect, Pulse), <filename>network-manager-l2tp</filename>,
<filename>network-manager-pptp</filename>,
<filename>network-manager-strongswan</filename>,
<filename>network-manager-libreswan</filename>,
<filename>network-manager-fortisslvpn</filename>,
<filename>network-manager-sstp</filename> and
<filename>network-manager-ssh</filename>. The <guilabel>VPN type</guilabel>
list marks the ones that are not installed.
</para>
<para>
For every type the <guilabel>Gateway</guilabel>, user name and password
are shown as fields; OpenVPN also has its authentication type,
certificates, keys, port, TCP and device type. <guilabel>Other
Options</guilabel> lists every other option of the plugin by name, so all
of its features can be used; the plugin's documentation lists them. The
easiest way to set up a VPN is usually <guibutton>Import VPN...</guibutton>
with the configuration file from the VPN provider.
</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="edit-wireguard">
<title>WireGuard</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>WireGuard settings</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="editor-wireguard.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>WireGuard settings with a peer</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
&nm; supports WireGuard itself. Enter the <guilabel>Interface
name</guilabel> (for example <userinput>wg0</userinput>) and the
<guilabel>Private key</guilabel>; <guibutton>Generate</guibutton> creates a
new key (with <command>wg</command> from wireguard-tools) and shows the
public key to give to the other side. Each <guilabel>Peer</guilabel> has a
public key, the allowed IPs that are routed to it, an endpoint
(host:port), a keepalive interval and an optional preshared key. The
addresses of the tunnel go in the IPv4 and IPv6 tabs (method Manual).
A WireGuard <filename>.conf</filename> file can also be imported.
</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="edit-other">
<title>Mobile Broadband, DSL and Virtual Devices</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Mobile Broadband</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>For GSM, UMTS, LTE and 5G modems: the
<guilabel>APN</guilabel> of your provider, user name and password if
needed, the SIM PIN, the network ID and whether roaming is allowed. CDMA
modems need the number, usually <userinput>#777</userinput>. Modems are
handled by ModemManager.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>DSL/PPPoE</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The Ethernet device the DSL modem is connected to, the
user name and password of the provider, an optional service name and PPP
options.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Bond</guilabel>, <guilabel>Team</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Combine several network cards into one, for more speed
or redundancy: the interface name, the mode (round-robin, active backup,
802.3ad and others) and link monitoring. The <guilabel>Ports</guilabel>
list holds a connection for each network card; <guibutton>Add...</guibutton>
creates one.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Bridge</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>A virtual switch, for example for virtual machines, with
STP and VLAN filtering settings and its ports.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>VLAN</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The parent interface and VLAN ID; the interface name is
suggested as <replaceable>parent</replaceable>.<replaceable>id</replaceable>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>InfiniBand</guilabel>, <guilabel>Dummy</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The transport mode and P_Key of InfiniBand, the interface
name of a dummy device.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Proxy</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>A proxy auto-configuration URL that &nm; hands to
applications which ask for it. TDE's own proxy settings are in the Control
Center under Internet &amp; Network &gt; Proxy.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="devices">
<title>Devices</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Devices tab</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="devices.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>A Wi-Fi device with its addresses and the networks in range</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
The <guilabel>Devices</guilabel> tab lists the network devices with their
type, state and connection. Below are the details of the selected device:
the reason when it failed or is unavailable, hardware, driver and
firmware, hardware address, speed, MTU, the IPv4 and IPv6 addresses,
gateway and DNS servers, the traffic, and for Wi-Fi devices the network,
signal, frequency and all networks in range with their channel and
access point address.
</para>
<para>
<guibutton>Disconnect</guibutton> disconnects the device until you
connect it again; <guibutton>Search for Networks</guibutton> scans for
Wi-Fi networks. <guilabel>Managed by NetworkManager</guilabel> hands a
device to &nm; or takes it away, <guilabel>Connect automatically</guilabel>
allows automatic connections on it.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="settings">
<title>Settings</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Settings tab</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="settings.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Global network settings</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Enable networking</guilabel>, <guilabel>Enable Wi-Fi</guilabel>, <guilabel>Enable mobile broadband</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Take effect at once.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Check Internet access</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>&nm;'s connectivity check, which finds out whether the
Internet can be reached and whether a captive portal wants you to sign
in. <guibutton>Check Now</guibutton> checks at once.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Host name</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The name of this computer.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Store new passwords in TDE Wallet</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The default storage of new passwords, see
<xref linkend="security-passwords"/>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Show notifications</guilabel>, <guilabel>Show all Wi-Fi networks in range</guilabel>, <guilabel>Start the tray icon at login</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Options of the tray applet.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>
Press <guibutton>Apply</guibutton> to save the host name and the
options.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="kcm">
<title>Control Center and System Settings</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The module in the Control Center</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="kcontrol.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Network Connections in the Trinity Control Center</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
The editor is also a module of the Trinity Control Center, under
<guimenuitem>Internet &amp; Network</guimenuitem> &gt; <guimenuitem>Network
Connections</guimenuitem>, of System Settings, under <guimenuitem>Network
&amp; Connectivity</guimenuitem> &gt; <guimenuitem>Network
Settings</guimenuitem>, and can be started alone with
<command>tdecmshell nmctl</command>. It has the same three tabs;
<guibutton>Apply</guibutton> saves the <guilabel>Settings</guilabel> tab,
connections are saved by their own dialogs.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="security">
<title>Passwords and Permissions</title>

<sect1 id="security-passwords">
<title>Where Passwords Are Stored</title>

<para>
Every password field has a list next to it with the storage, as in
nm-connection-editor and plasma-nm:
</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Store in TDE Wallet (this user)</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The password belongs to you. &nm; does not store it; it is
kept encrypted in &wallet; (the network wallet, folder
<filename>tde-nmctl</filename>) and the tray applet gives it to &nm; when
connecting. Only you can connect with it, and only while you are logged
in. This is the default.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Store for all users (unencrypted)</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>&nm; stores the password in its connection file under
<filename>/etc/NetworkManager/system-connections</filename>, readable only
by root. The connection also works before anyone logs in and for every
user. Changing such a connection usually needs the administrator's
password.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Ask every time</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The password is never stored; the tray asks for it each
time.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Not required</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>(VPN only) the plugin does not need this secret.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>
The first time a password is stored in or read from &wallet;, the wallet
asks for its own password (or, the first time ever, sets up the wallet).
The stored passwords can be seen and deleted with
<application>TDE Wallet Manager</application>.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="security-polkit">
<title>Administrator Rights (polkit)</title>
<para>
&nm; checks with &polkit; whether you may do what you ask. Usually a
logged in user may connect and disconnect, turn Wi-Fi on and off and
change connections that belong to them, while changing connections for
all users, the host name, or turning off networking needs the
administrator's password. A polkit authentication agent asks for it; in
TDE that is <application>polkit-agent-tde</application>, which has to be
running.
</para>
<para>
&tdenmctl; asks &polkit; beforehand (with polkit-tqt) and shows a note in
the editor when a change will need the password, or when you are not
allowed to make it. It also warns when no authentication agent is
running, because then the change would simply fail.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="troubleshooting">
<title>Troubleshooting</title>

<qandaset>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The editor says NetworkManager is not running.</para></question>
<answer><para>Start the service, for example with <command>systemctl
enable --now NetworkManager</command>. If another network program such as
ifupdown or systemd-networkd manages the device, &nm; leaves it alone: see
the <guilabel>Devices</guilabel> tab and
<filename>/etc/NetworkManager/NetworkManager.conf</filename>.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Saving fails with <quote>Not authorized</quote>.</para></question>
<answer><para>A polkit authentication agent is needed to type the
administrator's password. Make sure <command>polkit-agent-tde</command>
runs (it is started at login by the polkit-agent-tde package), or untick
<guilabel>All users may connect to this network</guilabel> to make the
connection yours.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>A Wi-Fi network asks for the password every time.</para></question>
<answer><para>The tray applet must be running, because it gives &nm; the
passwords from &wallet;. Also check that the password storage is not
<guilabel>Ask every time</guilabel>, and that &wallet; is enabled (Control
Center, Security &amp; Privacy, TDE Wallet).</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>A VPN type is marked <quote>not installed</quote>.</para></question>
<answer><para>Install the plugin package named in the list, for example
<filename>network-manager-openvpn</filename>, and restart &nm;.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Two network icons are in the panel.</para></question>
<answer><para>TDENetworkManager is also running. Choose to turn it off
when asked, or untick its autostart.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>How can I see what &nm; is doing?</para></question>
<answer><para><command>tde-nmctl --list</command> prints the devices,
networks and connections; <command>journalctl -u NetworkManager</command>
shows &nm;'s log.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
</qandaset>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="commands">
<title>Command Line</title>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><command>tde-nmctl</command> <option>--page</option> <replaceable>connections|devices|settings</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Opens the editor on a page.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><command>tde-nmctl</command> <option>--edit</option> <replaceable>name or UUID</replaceable> [<option>--tab</option> <replaceable>n</replaceable>]</term>
<listitem><para>Edits one connection.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><command>tde-nmctl</command> <option>--new</option> <replaceable>type</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Creates a connection: <userinput>ethernet</userinput>,
<userinput>wifi</userinput>, <userinput>vpn</userinput>,
<userinput>wireguard</userinput>, <userinput>bond</userinput>,
<userinput>bridge</userinput>, <userinput>team</userinput>,
<userinput>vlan</userinput>, <userinput>gsm</userinput>,
<userinput>cdma</userinput>, <userinput>pppoe</userinput>,
<userinput>infiniband</userinput>, <userinput>dummy</userinput>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><command>tde-nmctl</command> <option>--list</option>, <option>--show</option> <replaceable>connection</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Print the state or one connection's settings, without an
X display.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><command>tde-nmctl-tray</command> [<option>--popup</option>]</term>
<listitem><para>Starts the tray applet (and opens the popup).</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><command>tdecmshell nmctl</command></term>
<listitem><para>The Control Center module alone.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>See also the manual pages <citerefentry><refentrytitle>tde-nmctl</refentrytitle><manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>
and <citerefentry><refentrytitle>tde-nmctl-tray</refentrytitle><manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>.</para>
</chapter>

